    {
      "title": "Gaganyaan's first uncrewed mission likely to launch this year: Isro chief",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/gaganyaan-s-first-uncrewed-mission-likely-to-launch-this-year-isro-chief-126090400065_1.html",
      "description": "ISRO chief V Narayanan on Friday said that \"vigorous, systematic work,\" is on for the launch of the first uncrewed Gaganyaan mission during this calendar year.\nThe launch of the earth observation satellite (GSLV-F17/EOS-05) is the first success for ISRO during this financial year, Narayanan said adding, \"reward for hard work is more work.\"\nAccordingly, the ISRO team, along with industries and other organisations, is working on the first uncrewed Gaganyaan mission.\nIn his address following the successful launch of EOS-05, he said that activities were underway very \"vigorously and systematically,\" for the uncrewed mission launch during this calendar year.\nPraising Prime Minister Narendra Modi for his leadership, Narayanan said that six more launches, including the navigation satellite NVS-03, are targeted for this financial year.\nLater, addressing a press conference, the ISRO chief said the earth observation satellite's operational lifetime is nine years and dismissed speculations t",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-04T02:07:41"
    },
    {
      "title": "Isro launches India's first imaging satellite in geostationary orbit",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/isro-launches-india-s-first-imaging-satellite-in-geostationary-orbit-126090400054_1.html",
      "description": "ISRO on Friday made its way into history books yet again with the successful launch of the country's first ever imaging satellite in geo-orbit, placing the over 2,300 kg payload into a precise orbit using a GSLV rocket.\nFriday's launch marked the space agency's maiden success for the year 2026.\nThe EOS-05 satellite was successfully placed in the intended sub-geosynchronous transfer orbit, about 18 minutes after it lifted off from the spaceport here, ISRO announced.\nWhile several earth observation satellites operate from low earth orbit (LEO), the EOS-5 satellite is set to hover at an altitude of roughly 36,000 km above earth, which is the geosynchronous orbit and at that level, the satellite will match Earth's rotational speed.\nAt over 2,300 kg, this is the heaviest satellite ever to be carried by a GSLV and the satellite was perfectly ahead in its trajectory.\n\"The eye in the sky,\" as one of the senior scientists involved in the mission described the satellite, is set to provide .",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-04T02:02:22"
    }
